WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
Tool calls, steps, cost, and reliability across trials.

2
00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:19.459
Within a trajectory, check tool calls as four separate things, as the evals FAQ recommends: the name (right tool), the arguments (right values, which you can often check with code), the result (did the call succeed, and did the agent handle an error),

3
00:00:19.739 --> 00:00:23.344
and the resulting state (did it change what it should, and nothing else).

4
00:00:23.624 --> 00:00:25.843
Separate checks tell you where a failure starts.

5
00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:39.136
The FAQ’s transition failure matrix turns this into a diagnostic: rows are the last step that succeeded, columns are the step where things first went wrong, and the cell counts show where failures cluster.

6
00:00:39.416 --> 00:00:45.005
Early-stage failures cascade, so the hottest early cell is usually the best place to start.

7
00:00:45.285 --> 00:00:48.336
Track cost and steps per task alongside success.

8
00:00:48.616 --> 00:00:55.699
Two agents with the same success rate are not equivalent if one takes 40 tool calls and three times the tokens.

9
00:00:55.979 --> 00:01:03.573
Report median and a high percentile, because agent costs have long tails: a few runaway loops can dominate the bill.

10
00:01:03.853 --> 00:01:07.416
A step budget that ends the run is also a safety control.

11
00:01:07.696 --> 00:01:08.955
Then reliability.

12
00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:14.973
Agents are non-deterministic, so one trial per task overstates what users experience.

13
00:01:15.253 --> 00:01:17.088
Run several trials per task.

14
00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:24.365
pass@k is the chance at least one of k tries succeeds, which suits tools where a person can pick the best attempt.

15
00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:33.776
pass^k (read “pass hat k”) is the chance that all k tries succeed, which suits customer-facing agents, because every user is a new try.

16
00:01:34.056 --> 00:01:49.928
tau-bench introduced pass^k and estimates it per task from n trials with c successes as the number of ways to choose k successes divided by the number of ways to choose k trials, C(c, k) over C(n, k), averaged over tasks.

17
00:01:50.208 --> 00:02:00.917
Anthropic’s guide gives the intuition: an agent that succeeds 75% of the time succeeds three times in a row only about 42% of the time.
